BBO Capstone Project – Reflection

Overview

This project explored black-box optimisation through iterative query submission, with the objective of efficiently identifying high-performing regions of unknown functions. Over multiple rounds, my approach evolved from relatively simple stochastic search methods into a more structured, data-driven optimisation framework incorporating elements of surrogate modelling, clustering, dimensionality reduction and controlled exploration.

What began as a basic implementation gradually became a system focused on understanding the structure of the search space, rather than simply sampling it.

⸻

Initial Codebase

I began with a lightweight, custom-built framework centred around Kernel Ridge Regression (RBF kernel) paired with a simple Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) acquisition strategy.

Rather than relying on a full Bayesian optimisation library, I deliberately chose to build from scratch. This allowed for:
	•	Greater transparency over how decisions were made
	•	Flexibility to iterate and experiment with different components
	•	A deeper understanding of how exploration and exploitation interact

At this stage, candidate points were generated using random or Sobol sampling, and selection was driven purely by predicted mean and uncertainty.

While functional, this approach lacked structure and was highly sensitive to noise in early observations.

⸻

Evolution of the Approach

Early Iterations (Rounds 1–3)

The initial phase was exploratory and somewhat naïve. The model relied heavily on:
	•	Random or quasi-random sampling
	•	Basic UCB scoring
	•	No adjustment for noise or model confidence

This led to inconsistent performance. The model often overreacted to early data and prematurely focused on suboptimal regions.

⸻

Mid Iterations (Rounds 4–8)

The first meaningful improvements came from introducing stability and calibration into the model.

Key changes included:
	•	Cross-validation for hyperparameter tuning (alpha and gamma), improving surrogate accuracy
	•	A noise proxy, used to dynamically adjust exploration strength (kappa)
	•	Local sampling around top-performing points, increasing efficiency

These changes helped the model become more robust. Rather than reacting to individual observations, it began to recognise broader patterns in the data.

⸻

Later Iterations (Rounds 9–11)

At this stage, the focus shifted from accuracy to structure.

The most impactful addition was cluster-aware sampling:
	•	Agglomerative clustering was used to identify natural groupings in observed data
	•	Sampling was distributed across clusters based on performance and density
	•	Under-sampled clusters were prioritised for exploration

Alongside this, I introduced:
	•	Risk-adjusted scoring, penalising high-uncertainty or boundary-heavy candidates
	•	A decoder-style shortlist (top-k / top-p sampling) to reduce randomness in final selection

This marked a turning point. The optimisation process became more deliberate, balancing global exploration with targeted refinement.

⸻

Final Iterations (Rounds 12+)

In the final stages, I incorporated PCA-based dimensional structure into the search process.

This allowed the model to:
	•	Identify directions of highest variance (analogous to principal components)
	•	Bias sampling along informative directions
	•	Penalise movement into low-signal, noisy regions

Local sampling was also made anisotropic, meaning it adapted to the geometry of the data rather than applying uniform noise.

At this point, the system was no longer just sampling efficiently — it was actively modelling the shape of the problem space.

⸻

Final Results and Reflection

In the final rounds, performance improvements became smaller but more consistent. This suggested that the model had begun converging on strong regions of the search space.

If I were to restart or extend the project, I would:
	•	Introduce Gaussian Processes earlier for more principled uncertainty modelling
	•	Explore Thompson Sampling as an alternative to UCB
	•	Consider simplifying later-stage complexity, as some additions risked diminishing returns

⸻

Trade-offs and Key Decisions

The central challenge throughout the project was managing the exploration–exploitation trade-off.
	•	Early on, too much exploration led to inefficient use of queries
	•	Midway, excessive exploitation caused the model to become trapped in local optima
	•	Later, this balance was improved through:
	•	Noise-aware tuning of exploration (kappa)
	•	Cluster-based allocation of sampling effort
	•	PCA-guided reduction of uninformative exploration

Another key trade-off was between model complexity and robustness. While adding clustering, PCA and decoding improved performance, each layer increased system complexity and required careful calibration to avoid instability.

⸻

Key Learnings

Structured exploration outperforms randomness

The most important insight from this project was that effective optimisation is not about increasing randomness, but about making exploration more informed.

Techniques such as clustering and PCA allowed the model to:
	•	Identify meaningful regions in the search space
	•	Reduce redundant or noisy exploration
	•	Focus computational effort where it mattered most

⸻

Real-world relevance

These ideas extend beyond the capstone project.

In a real-world context (e.g. finance or portfolio management), similar principles apply:
	•	Identifying clusters of opportunity or behaviour
	•	Allocating resources under uncertainty
	•	Balancing risk against expected return

⸻

What surprised me most

Two things stood out:
	1.	How quickly simple methods plateau — early gains are easy, but sustained improvement requires structural insight
	2.	How powerful relatively simple tools can be — clustering and PCA, when applied thoughtfully, had a disproportionate impact on performance
